<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bolghar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bolgar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bolghar (Bolgar) was a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, south of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatarstan">Tatarstan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and a capital and trading centre of Volga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> from the 10th century. After the Mongol conquest it became an important city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, where coins were minted, and it flourished with stone mosques and mausoleums in the 14th century. Its ruins are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bolghar.jpg" alt="Bolghar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Indeikin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%91%D1%83%D0%BB%D0%B3%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B5_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%89%D0%B5_9.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
